E=[
(1,1898,"Sky","A red bar in the dark sky at Sedberg","A correspondent to Nature sees at Sedberg something in the sky like the red part of a rainbow, about 10 degrees long, though the sky was dark. Fort files it with other 'rainbow-like' objects seen that July.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 58-224","upon July 1, 1898, at Sedberg, he had seen in the sky"),
(3,1860,"Falls","A stunned snake in South Granville","During a heavy shower at South Granville, New York, a man hears a peculiar sound at his feet and finds a gray snake about a foot long lying as if stunned by a fall. It then comes to life.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 3-112","during a heavy shower, July 3, 1860, he heard a peculiar sound at his feet"),
(4,1832,"Cosmos","Lights in the Mare Crisium","Webb records a brilliant display of minute dots and streaks of light in the Mare Crisium on the Moon. Fort says he has half a dozen similar records in about 120 years, always with the Mare in darkness.","NL","New Lands, citing Astronomical Register, 20-165","a brilliant display of minute dots and streaks of light, in the Mare Crisium, July 4, 1832"),
(5,1877,"Falls","Clinkers strew a Kilburn street","During a storm a Kilburn street is 'literally strewn' with about two bushels of clinkers, from walnut-sized to the size of a man's hand. The Kilburn Times reported it and G. T. Symons repeated it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Kilburn Times, July 7, 1877","Clinkers, said to have fallen, during a storm, at Kilburn, July 5, 1877"),
(6,1874,"Sky","A giant trumpet over Oaxaca","At Oaxaca, Mexico, something like a gigantic trumpet is seen suspended vertically and oscillating gently, estimated 425 feet long. It is visible five or six minutes. Fort cites Scientific American Supplement.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Sci. Am. Sup., 6-2365","something like a gigantic trumpet, suspended, vertical, oscillating gently"),
(8,1886,"Falls","Snails fall at Redruth","In a heavy thunderstorm, small land snails strew roads and fields near Redruth, Cornwall, so many that they are gathered by the hatful. None are seen to fall. Science Gossip reported it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Science Gossip, 1886-238","small snails, of a land species, had fallen near Redruth, Cornwall, July 8, 1886"),
(9,1853,"Sky","Red points over Urrugne","A great number of red points are seen in the sky at Urrugne, France. Fort lists it among things that may have been seeds, and may not.","NL","New Lands, citing An. Soc. Met. de France, 1853-227","A great number of red points in the sky of Urrugne, July 9, 1853"),
(11,1864,"Falls","Ice, not hail, at Pontiac","In a storm at Pontiac, Canada, 'pieces of ice, from half an inch to over two inches in diameter' fall, not hailstones. The Canadian Naturalist described it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Canadian Naturalist, 2-1-308","Description of a storm, at Pontiac, Canada, July 11, 1864"),
(12,1883,"Falls","A brick of ice on Chicago","A lump of ice the size of a brick falls on Chicago, weighing two pounds. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review in a list of ice falls, with far larger ones elsewhere.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, July 1883","lump of ice size of a brick; weight two pounds, Chicago, July 12, 1883"),
(13,1896,"Cosmos","A light moving near Saturn","A correspondent to the English Mechanic watches through a telescope from ten until after 11.15 p.m. a luminous object moving near Saturn. He sees it pass several small stars.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 63-496","upon July 13, 1896, he saw, through his telescope"),
(15,1757,"Mysteries","Hoof-like marks on the sands of Penzance","After the earthquake, marks like hoof prints, but not crescent-shaped, are found over more than 100 square yards of the sands at Penzance, Cornwall. Fort sets it with marks on beaches after quakes elsewhere.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Phil. Trans., 50-500","After the quake of July 15, 1757, upon the sands of Penzance"),
(16,1850,"Mysteries","Wagons rumbling at Bulwick Rectory","A sound 'as of wagons' is heard for over an hour without stopping at Bulwick Rectory, Northamptonshire. On the 19th a black rain fell. Fort cites Timb's Year Book.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Timb's Year Book, 1851-270","July 16, 1850, Bulwick Rectory, Northampton, England"),
(17,1860,"Falls","The fall at Dhurmsalla","Something falls at Dhurmsalla, India, in the summer of fishes and red substances reported from there. Fort gives this date from the Times and adds that 'it' is persistently called a meteorite.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing London Times, Dec. 26, 1860 (Fort's date)","Something then fell from somewhere, July 17, 1860, at Dhurmsalla"),
(18,1881,"Mysteries","Sulphur vapour from a tornado cloud","In the tornado at Americus, Georgia, 'a strange sulphurous vapor was emitted from the cloud.' Fort notes that things from beyond Earth often have a sulphurous odour.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Finley, tornado studies","in the tornado at Americus, Georgia, July 18, 1881"),
(19,1912,"Falls","The Holbrook stones","At Holbrook, Arizona, about 50 miles from Meteor Crater, a loud detonation comes with one of the most remarkable falls of stones recorded. Fort cites the American Journal of Science.","NL","New Lands, citing Amer. Jour. Sci., 4-34-437","Upon July 19, 1912, at Holbrook, Arizona"),
(20,1829,"Falls","Fishes at Moradabad","A fall of fishes at Moradabad, India, is listed among the Indian fish-falls Dr. Buist collected in the Living Age and the Linnean Society's Transactions.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Lin. Soc. Trans., 16-764","Moradabad, India, July 20, 1829"),
(21,1887,"Falls","Wingless ants fall at Nancy","An enormous fall of ants, 'most of them wingless', hits Nancy, France. Fort uses the wings, which swarming ants would have, to argue the ants fell as larvae.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 36-349","Enormous fall of ants, Nancy, France, July 21, 1887"),
(24,1818,"Falls","Jagged ice that smelled of sulphur","Jagged pieces of ice with a strong sulphurous odour fall in Orkney. Fort cites the Transactions of the Royal Society of Edinburgh and compares coke and sulphur at Mortree, France in 1887.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Trans. Roy. Soc. Edin., 9-187","A fall of jagged pieces of ice, Orkney, July 24, 1818"),
(25,1850,"Falls","Fishes cover the ground at Rajkote","After one of the heaviest rainfalls on record, 'the ground was found literally covered with fishes' at Rajkote, India. Fort challenges the word 'found', and cites All the Year Round and Dr. Buist.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing All the Year Round, 8-255","Rajkote, India, July 25, 1850"),
(26,1819,"Cosmos","Two bodies cross the Sun together","Gruithuisen observes two bodies crossing the Sun together. Fort adds it to the 'disregarded' observations that Le Verrier's critics left out.","BD","Book of the Damned","Observation, of July 26, 1819, by Gruthinson"),
(27,1875,"Falls","Damp hay falls at Monkstown","Small masses of damp hay fall at Monkstown, Ireland. Dr. J. W. Moore blamed a nearby whirlwind in the Dublin Daily Express, but Fort says the data make that unacceptable.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Dublin Daily Express","upon July 27, 1875, small masses of damp hay had fallen at Monkstown, Ireland"),
(28,1874,"Falls","Plant debris on Toulouse hailstones","Vegetable debris, not only at the nucleus but frozen on the surfaces of large hailstones, falls at Toulouse, France. Fort cites La Science Pour Tous.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing La Science Pour Tous, 1874-270","frozen upon the surfaces of large hailstones, at Toulouse, France, July 28, 1874"),
(29,1878,"Cosmos","Vulcan at the eclipse","During the total eclipse, Prof. Watson at Rawlins, Wyoming and Prof. Swift at Denver, Colorado report two shining objects at a considerable distance from the Sun. Fort treats it as a lost chapter of Vulcan.","BD","Book of the Damned","they had seen two shining objects at a considerable distance from the sun"),
(30,1880,"Sky","Three lights in a ravine near St. Petersburg","A large spherical light and two smaller ones move along a ravine in the evening near St. Petersburg. They are visible for three minutes and vanish without noise. L'Annee Scientifique reported it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Annee Scientifique, 1888-66","Observed near St. Petersburg, July 30, 1880, in the evening"),
(31,1813,"Sky","Flashes over Tottenham","On a clear night flashes of light appear in the sky of Tottenham, near London. They were attributed to a storm at Hastings 65 miles away. Fort cites the Year Book of Facts.","NL","New Lands, citing Year Book of Facts, 1853-272","flashes of light in the sky of Tottenham, near London"),
]
